To generate a table of contents (TOC) in a LaTeX document, you can use the `toc` command from the `tocloft` package. Here's an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tocloft}

\begin{document}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
\subsection{Background}
\subsubsection{Problem Statement}
\subsubsection{Literature Review}
\subsubsection{Methodology}
\subsubsection{Results}
\subsubsection{Discussion}
\subsubsection{Conclusion}

\end{document}
```

In this example, we first load the `tocloft` package using `\usepackage{tocloft}`. Then, we define a new section called "Introduction" and include its subsections under it. Each subsection is defined within its own section.

This will create a TOC with the following structure:

1. Introduction
2. Background
3. Literature Review
4. Methodology
5. Results
6. Discussion
7. Conclusion

You can customize the content of each section by modifying the subsections and their subheadings as needed.

